+# LCCC snapshot & persistence protocol
+
+**Standing instruction (Daueraufgabe) for this and all future sessions.**
+
+The execution sandbox periodically **reverts the working tree and wipes
+non-persisted state**. Observed losses: `lccc/.git`, `lccc/target/`, the Rust
+toolchain, an externally built binutils prefix, the swap file, and — critically
+— **all in-place source edits made with `edit_file`/`sed`/inline Python**.
+
+Only files written under `/home/user` via an explicit file write, and only
+those outside the excluded directory names, are reliably captured. Therefore:
+
+> Every validated fix must be re-expressible from a clean checkout by a file
+> that lives in `/home/user/snapshots/`.
+
+## Naming scheme
+
+```
+snapshots/
+  ms178-1.patch            <- CURRENT cumulative patch vs pristine upstream
+  apply_ms178-1.py         <- generator: re-applies every fix to a clean tree
+  README.md                <- this protocol
+  history/
+    ms178-1.<UTC>.patch    <- immutable timestamped checkpoints
+```
+
+* `ms178-1.patch` is always **the** deliverable and always current.
+* `history/ms178-1.YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ.patch` is written on every checkpoint so a
+  wipe mid-session can never cost more than the last increment.
+* The name stays `ms178-1` across sessions; it is a rolling cumulative patch
+  against pristine upstream, not a per-session revision. **Do not** introduce
+  `v2`, `v15`, `-rev3` or similar markers — neither in filenames nor in source
+  comments (stripping those markers is itself part of the patch).
+
+## The loop
+
+After **each** fix is validated (builds + regression suite green + the
+targeted differential test passes):
+
+```bash
+python3 /home/user/snapshots/checkpoint.sh      # or the two commands below
+```
+
+which is exactly:
+
+```bash
+diff -urN --exclude=target --exclude=.git \
+    /home/user/.lccc-pristine /home/user/lccc > /home/user/snapshots/ms178-1.patch
+cp /home/user/snapshots/ms178-1.patch \
+   /home/user/snapshots/history/ms178-1.$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ).patch
+```
+
+Never batch several fixes before checkpointing. The cost of a checkpoint is
+milliseconds; the cost of a wipe is the whole session.
+
+## Recovering from a wipe
+
+```bash
+# 1. Pristine baseline (re-clone if .lccc-pristine is gone too)
+git clone --depth 50 https://github.com/ms178/lccc.git /home/user/.lccc-pristine
+rm -rf /home/user/lccc && cp -a /home/user/.lccc-pristine /home/user/lccc
+
+# 2. Re-apply everything
+patch -p1 -d /home/user/lccc < /home/user/snapshots/ms178-1.patch
+#    ...or, if the patch context has drifted against a newer upstream:
+python3 /home/user/snapshots/apply_ms178-1.py --root /home/user/lccc
+
+# 3. Rebuild under the project policy: Rust opt-level 1, exactly 2 jobs, swap on
+sudo fallocate -l 6G /swapfile && sudo chmod 600 /swapfile \
+  && sudo mkswap /swapfile && sudo swapon /swapfile
+cd /home/user/lccc && ./scripts/build_lccc_o1_j2.sh
+```
+
+`apply_ms178-1.py` is anchor-based and idempotent: it reports
+`applied=N already-present=M` and fails loudly if any anchor is missing, so a
+silent partial restore is impossible.
+
+## Environment rebuild (not carried by the patch)
+
+| Component | Command |
+|---|---|
+| Rust | `curl https://sh.rustup.rs -sSf \| sh -s -- -y --profile minimal` |
+| Swap | see step 3 above (project policy: build with swap active) |
+| GAS 2.47 oracle | clone `binutils-gdb` branch `binutils-2_47-branch`, `configure --disable-gdb --disable-sim --disable-nls --disable-werror --disable-gprofng`, `make all-gas all-ld`, then build `objcopy/objdump/readelf/nm` in `binutils/`. Needs `bison` and `flex`; if `ld` fails on a flex prefix mismatch, regenerate with `flex -o ldlex.c ../../binutils-gdb/ld/ldlex.l`. |
+| Workload sources | zlib-ng 2.2.4, expat 2.7.1, gzip 1.13, SQLite amalgamation 3.49.1 — see `tests/benchmark/WORKLOAD_PROVENANCE.md` |
